New York is answering the growing calls  for police reform following the deadly  rest of George Floyd on Friday Governor  Andrew Cuomo signed a package of  accountability laws they include a ban  on police chokeholds and legislation to  allow the release of officers long  withheld disciplinary records the change  comes after the revelation that one of  the fired Minneapolis officers have been  accused of misconduct 17 times before  Floyd's death but the allegations of  excessive force went far beyond just one  officer and resulted in millions of  dollars in settlements Jeff the case has  that part of the story in 2012 Zach king  was leaving a Minneapolis nightclub when  he was stopped by the police he had a  handgun that he was legally permitted to  carry his I hate what's that bulge on  your on your waist I shot my hands I  hate I have a conceal and carry and  that's my gun my weapon and like six  officers just came from out of  everywhere and they got their knee on me  like almost like George Floyd I couldn't  breathe  King was hospitalized with a concussion  his face swollen with cuts and bruises I  didn't do anything to anybody and I was  violated King sued the department which  argued he didn't tell them he had a gun  permit until later he got a 122 thousand  dollar settlement ultimately who's  paying the tab for those settlements  residents of the city of Minneapolis  we've seen similar culture of abuse and  other large cities across the country  since 2003 Minneapolis has paid out  forty five million dollars in  settlements this year alone Los Angeles  has paid more than six million in  settlements Chicago more than 14 million  New York since 2015 more than a billion  dollars Zak Kings as it was officer  Joshua steward who led the assault  against him since the incident a CBS  News investigation found nine more  misconduct complaints have been made  against him none resulting in discipline  what do you make of that  his is pitiful his line is ridiculous  the Minneapolis Police Department told  CBS News that previous conduct and  nature of current offense  are factors that can be figured into  discipline King says not all police are  bad his little brother as a cop they  just need to be held accountable if you  sit back and watch your back hop do bad  stuff you're a bad cop two Minneapolis  PD still rebuilding its reputation after  George Floyd's death tell us that in the  Zach king case the officer at the center  of the case is still on the job and if  Derrick Chauvin is convicted in  connection with the death of George  Floyd he could still be eligible to  receive a pension that's worth more than  a million dollars receive yes this  morning Saturday Jeff buguese
 

